msgstr "What volume unit pairs with kilograms for SI density?"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1505
msgid "When a chart lists dose per 10,000 litres, convert your pool volume to litres, divide by 10,000, then multiply by the listed dose. Write the unit beside every intermediate number."
msgstr "When a chart lists dose per 10,000 litres, convert your pool volume to litres, divide by 10,000, then multiply by the listed dose. Write the unit beside every intermediate number."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1069
msgid "When a part fails inspection after a unit change, audit the conversion before auditing the operator. Many escapes are metrological, not motivational. Keep a short conversion verification coupon on first-article inspections whenever drawings change unit systems."
msgstr "When a part fails inspection after a unit change, audit the conversion before auditing the operator. Many escapes are metrological, not motivational. Keep a short conversion verification coupon on first-article inspections whenever drawings change unit systems."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1337
msgid "When a quote jumps after a “unit clarification,” ask for the area in both m² and ft². Transparent dual totals catch silent conversion errors."
msgstr "When a quote jumps after a “unit clarification,” ask for the area in both m² and ft². Transparent dual totals catch silent conversion errors."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2094
msgid "When a US recipe says “ounces” for solids, it usually means weight ounces. When it says “fluid ounces” or uses cup marks on a jug, it means volume. Read the word “fluid” carefully."
msgstr "When a US recipe says “ounces” for solids, it usually means weight ounces. When it says “fluid ounces” or uses cup marks on a jug, it means volume. Read the word “fluid” carefully."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2177
msgid "When a US telehealth portal rejects a weight, check whether it expects lb and you typed kg (or the reverse). Unit mismatch looks like physiologically impossible input."
msgstr "When a US telehealth portal rejects a weight, check whether it expects lb and you typed kg (or the reverse). Unit mismatch looks like physiologically impossible input."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2034
msgid "When an overseas seller lists m² and your contractor thinks in ft², send both totals. Dual reporting catches silent conversion errors faster than arguing about a single number."
msgstr "When an overseas seller lists m² and your contractor thinks in ft², send both totals. Dual reporting catches silent conversion errors faster than arguing about a single number."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1178
msgid "When audience and standard conflict, publish both: primary unit for compliance, secondary for local clarity, with an explicit conversion note. Dual labelling beats silent assumption. Make the primary unit visually dominant so readers know which value wins if rounding differs."
msgstr "When audience and standard conflict, publish both: primary unit for compliance, secondary for local clarity, with an explicit conversion note. Dual labelling beats silent assumption. Make the primary unit visually dominant so readers know which value wins if rounding differs."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:233
msgid "When binary floats are still fine"
msgstr "When binary floats are still fine"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:154
msgid "When comparing converter outputs, tiny last-digit differences often come from rounding policy, not from different physical constants. Check whether both tools use the international inch, the same acre definition, and the same display precision."
msgstr "When comparing converter outputs, tiny last-digit differences often come from rounding policy, not from different physical constants. Check whether both tools use the international inch, the same acre definition, and the same display precision."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1043
msgid "When converting a nominal with ± tolerance, convert the limits (upper and lower) and then restate nominal and tolerance, or convert nominal and tolerance with consistent rounding rules that never shrink the allowed zone unintentionally."
msgstr "When converting a nominal with ± tolerance, convert the limits (upper and lower) and then restate nominal and tolerance, or convert nominal and tolerance with consistent rounding rules that never shrink the allowed zone unintentionally."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:599
msgid "When converting area, square the length factor. When converting volume, cube it. 1 m = 100 cm implies 1 m² = 10 000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. Applying the linear factor to an area value underconverts by ×100 in this example."
msgstr "When converting area, square the length factor. When converting volume, cube it. 1 m = 100 cm implies 1 m² = 10 000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. Applying the linear factor to an area value underconverts by ×100 in this example."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1261
msgid "When converting from feet and inches to metric, convert the whole height to inches first, then to centimetres, and round once at the end. Rounding each step separately can invent a centimetre you did not measure."
msgstr "When converting from feet and inches to metric, convert the whole height to inches first, then to centimetres, and round once at the end. Rounding each step separately can invent a centimetre you did not measure."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:451
msgid "When converting scientific length units, prefer explicit scientific notation alongside prefixed units in notebooks: 2.5 µm = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Dual writing catches transcription errors."
msgstr "When converting scientific length units, prefer explicit scientific notation alongside prefixed units in notebooks: 2.5 µm = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. Dual writing catches transcription errors."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2053
msgid "When do I add waste factor?"
msgstr "When do I add waste factor?"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:835
msgid "When estimating permeable surface for drainage fees, keep everything in m² or ft² consistently; switching mid-spreadsheet is how permit numbers go wrong."
msgstr "When estimating permeable surface for drainage fees, keep everything in m² or ft² consistently; switching mid-spreadsheet is how permit numbers go wrong."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1893
msgid "When in doubt, store seconds as integers (or milliseconds as integers) and format for humans at the edge. Integer bases avoid decimal-hour ambiguity entirely."
msgstr "When in doubt, store seconds as integers (or milliseconds as integers) and format for humans at the edge. Integer bases avoid decimal-hour ambiguity entirely."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:502
msgid "When plans mix units, convert the drawing once into the unit your tools display. Tape measures in the US often show inches; many metric tapes show centimetres and millimetres. Dual-scale tapes help but still require attention to which edge you read."
msgstr "When plans mix units, convert the drawing once into the unit your tools display. Tape measures in the US often show inches; many metric tapes show centimetres and millimetres. Dual-scale tapes help but still require attention to which edge you read."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:311
msgid "When publishing data, state the scale in every table and plot axis. Dual-scale axes are fine for public communication if both are computed from one master dataset."
msgstr "When publishing data, state the scale in every table and plot axis. Dual-scale axes are fine for public communication if both are computed from one master dataset."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:992
msgid "When reading older UK material, assume imperial gallons unless told otherwise. When reading US material, assume US liquid measures unless a metric cup is specified."
msgstr "When reading older UK material, assume imperial gallons unless told otherwise. When reading US material, assume US liquid measures unless a metric cup is specified."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1446
msgid "When renting a car abroad, note whether the trip computer shows km or miles. Resetting averages mid-trip with the wrong mental unit produces nonsense cost estimates."
msgstr "When renting a car abroad, note whether the trip computer shows km or miles. Resetting averages mid-trip with the wrong mental unit produces nonsense cost estimates."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:617
msgid "When results look surprising, ask whether you converted units or accidentally converted quantities. That single question saves projects. A second quick check is geometric: does the number imply a depth, a thickness, or a coverage rate that was never stated? Missing geometry usually means someone treated area as volume."
msgstr "When results look surprising, ask whether you converted units or accidentally converted quantities. That single question saves projects. A second quick check is geometric: does the number imply a depth, a thickness, or a coverage rate that was never stated? Missing geometry usually means someone treated area as volume."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1948
msgid "When scientists choose Julian years"
msgstr "When scientists choose Julian years"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1738
msgid "When sharing driving shifts with someone from another country, call out both units for the first hour (“90 km/h, about 56 mph”). Shared speech prevents one driver from “correcting” the other into a ticket."
msgstr "When sharing driving shifts with someone from another country, call out both units for the first hour (“90 km/h, about 56 mph”). Shared speech prevents one driver from “correcting” the other into a ticket."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1278
msgid "When sharing height internationally, prefer centimetres plus an optional feet-and-inches gloss. Dual labelling prevents silent unit assumptions in email and chat."
msgstr "When sharing height internationally, prefer centimetres plus an optional feet-and-inches gloss. Dual labelling prevents silent unit assumptions in email and chat."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1396
msgid "When sharing recipes online, include both scales. Your international readers should not have to guess which “gas mark” culture you meant."
msgstr "When sharing recipes online, include both scales. Your international readers should not have to guess which “gas mark” culture you meant."

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:391
msgid "When sharing your own recipes online, publish millilitres and grams alongside cups. Future readers in every country will thank you, and you will reduce support questions that are really unit questions."
msgstr "When sharing your own recipes online, publish millilitres and grams alongside cups. Future readers in every country will thank you, and you will reduce support questions that are really unit questions."

